t fields, I'll design RunDiffTest to accept goBinary and refBinary as parameters. Now I'm defining the DiffResult struct to hold comparison results with fields for match status, input arguments, and the normalized outputs and exit codes from both binaries.\n\nDefining DiffResult fields...\n\nBut I'm realizing RunDiffTest can't use testing.T methods like TempDir() or Fatalf() since it won't have access to *testing.T, which complicates how to handle the WorkDir. Let me reconsider the requirements more carefully to see how this should actually work.\n\nLooking back at the spec, RunDiffTest is a pure function that takes a DiffTest and returns a DiffResult—no testing.T involved. And DiffResult needs to capture whether outputs match, the input arguments, expected vs actual output, and which streams diverged. So I need to refactor the existing runBinary helper to work without testing.T, or create a new version that handles the binary execution and comparison logic independently.\n\nDefining the DiffResult struct... Defining the struct fields...\n\nI need to create a separate execution function that doesn't rely on testing.T, so I'll write an `execBinary` helper that captures output and returns errors directly instead of using fatal assertions. This function will set up a context with timeout, configure the command with the provided arguments and environment, and handle the execution independently.\n\nFinishing the error handling...\n\nNow I'm setting up the `RunDiffTest` function, which takes two binaries and a test case, then creates a temporary working directory if one wasn't provided.\n\nBuilding the environment variables and executing both the reference and Go binaries with the same arguments and stdin, then applying any normalizers to their output streams to prepare for comparison. I'm finishing the comparison logic by checking if the exit codes match, then returning the result. But now I'm realizing there's duplication between this and the `runBinary` function—the Go